{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9bf7096c6665b62e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tBEYOND PROMPT ADHERENCE:\n2\tAUDITING ATTRIBUTE-LEVEL VOICE CONTROL IN SPEECH GENERATION\n3\tXianhao Zhou1,∗\n4\t\n5\tJianghao Wu2\n6\t\n7\t1\n8\t2\n9\t\n10\tSchool of Mechanical and Electrical Engineering, UESTC, Chengdu, China\n11\tFaculty of Information Technology, Monash University, Melbourne, Australia\n12\t∗\n13\tCorresponding author: intelland2024@163.com\n14\t\n15\tarXiv:2608.00545v1 [cs.SD] 1 Aug 2026\n16\t\n17\tABSTRACT\n18\tNatural-language descriptions have become a flexible interface for\n19\tcontrolling generated speech. Existing evaluations largely assess\n20\twhether an output matches a prompt, but prompt matching alone\n21\tdoes not reveal whether characteristics outside the intended change\n22\tremain stable. We examine this distinction through a controlled\n23\tpaired audit of three speech-generation systems: CosyVoice3, VoxCPM2, and Fish-Speech-S2. The evaluation contains 5,940 outputs\n24\tspanning six reference speakers, ten texts, three random seeds, and\n25\televen conditions. Using acoustic, prosodic, content, and speaker\n26\tmeasurements, we find that responses in the expected target direction are frequently accompanied by changes outside descriptorspecific signal-level target sets. This pattern remains among outputs whose target response exceeds baseline seed variation, and the\n27\taccompanying changes differ substantially across systems. We further introduce VoDER-Cal, a training-free candidate selector that\n28\tretains sufficiently strong target responses while favoring smaller\n29\toff-target deviations. A three-candidate pool raises the joint success rate from 4.8% under single-sample direct generation to approximately 14% for all candidate-selection policies. Within the\n30\tmatched three-candidate budget, VoDER-Cal reduces held-out offtarget deviation from 0.344 under target-only selection to 0.276 and\n31\timproves listener-rated preservation. Preservation-sensitive evaluation therefore complements prompt-adherence evaluation, while\n32\tcandidate reranking offers a practical inference-time improvement.\n33\tCode, configuration files, and analysis scripts are available at https:\n34\t//github.com/intelland/VoDER.\n35\tIndex Terms— controllable speech generation, instructionfollowing TTS, voice descriptors, prompt adherence, acoustic\n36\tanalysis, inference-time reranking\n37\t1. INTRODUCTION\n38\tNatural language allows users to control generated speech without\n39\trelying on predefined style labels or low-level acoustic parameters. A\n40\tdesired voice can be described using attributes such as deep, bright, or\n41\trough, or through more detailed free-form instructions. PromptTTS,\n42\tInstructTTS, PromptTTS 2, PromptStyle, and PromptTTS++ demonstrated the feasibility of using text descriptions to control speaking style and voice characteristics [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]. More recent systems combine natural-language control with reference conditioning, zero-shot voice generation, and open-vocabulary instructions\n43\t[6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11].\n44\tEvaluation has largely focused on whether generated speech\n45\tis compatible with the requested description. Common measures\n46\tinclude prompt or style preference, naturalness, intelligibility,\n47\t\n48\tand speaker similarity, while recent benchmarks directly assess\n49\tinstruction-following fidelity [12, 13]. These evaluations reveal\n50\twhether a system responds to a prompt, but not whether it changes\n51\tthe requested attribute while preserving other characteristics of the\n52\tvoice. Prompt adherence and attribute-specific control are therefore\n53\trelated but distinct capabilities.\n54\tConsider a request for a deep voice. Lowering fundamental frequency is a plausible response, but the resulting speech may also\n55\tbecome slower, quieter, spectrally darker, or less similar to the reference speaker. Such an output may sound more compatible with the\n56\tword deep, yet fail to provide a localized and reproducible edit. This\n57\tdistinction matters when users want to preserve a reference voice,\n58\tcombine several controls, or apply the same modification consistently\n59\tacross speakers and texts. We therefore ask:\n60\tWhen a system responds to a voice descriptor, what\n61\tchanges outside the requested attribute?\n62\tWe operationalize this question with descriptor-specific signal-level\n63\ttarget sets defined before the final evaluation. Measurements outside\n64\tthe corresponding set are treated as off-target for the audit.\n65\tWe study this question through paired generation. For the same\n66\tsystem, reference speaker, text, and nominal random seed, we compare a neutral output with a descriptor-conditioned output. We evaluate three independently developed systems using a shared matrix\n67\tof speakers, texts, seeds, descriptors, and controls. Acoustic and\n68\tprosodic measurements quantify target and off-target movement,\n69\twhile ASR and speaker embeddings assess content and speaker preservation.\n70\tThe results reveal a gap between descriptor response and preservation. Systems often move in the expected target direction, particularly\n71\tfor deep, while simultaneously changing several non-target characteristics. The response profiles differ substantially across systems:\n72\tthe same descriptor may be realized through different combinations\n73\tof pitch, timing, energy, and spectral change. The pattern persists\n74\tafter restricting the analysis to outputs whose target response exceeds\n75\tbaseline seed variation. An auxiliary listening study finds that both\n76\trequested and accompanying non-target changes can be perceptually\n77\tnoticeable.\n78\tCandidate variability provides an opportunity to improve this\n79\tbehavior at inference time. Samples generated for the same request\n80\toften express the target attribute with different amounts of collateral movement. We therefore introduce VoDER-Cal, a training-free\n81\tcandidate selector that retains sufficiently strong target responses\n82\tand ranks feasible candidates by off-target deviation. It requires neither generator retraining nor access to internal model representations.\n83\tThe candidate pool chiefly improves the availability of feasible outputs, while VoDER-Cal improves preservation relative to target-only\n84\tselection under the same generation budget.\n85\t\n86\t\fExpected attribute control\n87\t\n88\tInput speech\n89\t\n90\tDepth/pitch\n91\t\n92\tChanged\n93\t\n94\tDepth/pitch\n95\t\n96\tChanged\n97\t\n98\tSpeaking rate\n99\t\n100\tPreserved\n101\t\n102\tSpeaking rate\n103\t\n104\tChanged\n105\t\n106\tLoudness\n107\t\n108\tPreserved\n109\t\n110\tLoudness\n111\t\n112\tChanged\n113\t\n114\tVoice quality\n115\t\n116\tPreserved\n117\t\n118\tVoice quality\n119\t\n120\tChanged\n121\t\n122\tSpeaker identity\n123\t\n124\tPreserved\n125\t\n126\tSpeaker identity\n127\t\n128\tPreserved\n129\t\n130\tDescriptor prompt\n131\t\n132\t“Use a deeper voice”\n133\t\n134\tObserved prompt response\n135\t\n136\tFig. 1. Prompt response and a localized attribute edit answer different questions. The paired audit measures the requested change together with\n137\tmovement outside a descriptor-specific signal-level target set. VoDER-Cal uses candidate variability to retain the target response while favoring\n138\tsmaller off-target movement.\n139\tOur contributions are:\n140\t• a paired, preservation-sensitive audit based on matched neutral\n141\tand descriptor-conditioned generations;\n142\t• a cross-system study of 5,940 outputs with request-level statistical\n143\tinference; and\n144\t• VoDER-Cal, a training-free candidate selector evaluated against\n145\tsingle-sample, matched-budget, and oracle baselines.\n146\t2. RELATED WORK\n147\t\n148\tevaluation benchmark [12], while SPAM develops a human-aligned\n149\tmeasure of prompt adherence [13].\n150\tThese evaluations primarily measure semantic or perceptual\n151\tagreement with the requested description, together with general generation quality and validity. They do not directly test whether characteristics outside the requested attribute remain stable relative to a\n152\tmatched baseline. Our paired audit adds this preservation-sensitive\n153\tdimension by separating target response from off-target movement.\n154\tThe analysis is behavioral: it characterizes changes in generated\n155\toutputs and does not infer whether the underlying models contain\n156\tdisentangled internal representations.\n157\t\n158\t2.1. Natural-language control of speech\n159\tControllable speech generation has traditionally relied on either\n160\tglobal style representations or explicitly defined acoustic factors.\n161\tReference-based prosody transfer and global style tokens encode\n162\tspeaking characteristics in learned latent representations [14, 15],\n163\twhile architectures such as FastSpeech 2 expose duration, pitch, and\n164\tenergy as explicit variance controls [16]. These approaches reflect\n165\ttwo different forms of control: matching a holistic style representation\n166\tand manipulating an identifiable speech factor.\n167\tNatural-language prompting provides a more flexible alternative\n168\tby allowing users to describe a desired speaking style or voice directly.\n169\tPromptTTS maps textual descriptions to speaking styles [1], while\n170\tInstructTTS studies expressive instruction following and reduces leakage among style, speaker, and linguistic content [2]. PromptTTS 2\n171\tand PromptTTS++ further expand text-described voice variation and\n172\tspeaker control [3, 5]. ControlSpeech combines reference-based\n173\tspeaker cloning with language-guided style control [6]. More recent\n174\tsystems extend natural-language control toward open-vocabulary\n175\tinstructions, zero-shot generation, and more structured speech representations [10, 11, 17, 18]. Our work studies a complementary\n176\tquestion: when these systems respond to a voice description, how\n177\tspecifically is the requested attribute changed?\n178\t\n179\t2.3. Inference-time candidate selection\n180\tMulti-candidate generation and reranking provide a training-free way\n181\tto improve the reliability of speech generation. In zero-shot TTS,\n182\tBest-of-N inference generates several candidates and uses an ASRbased verifier to select the output whose recognized transcript most\n183\tclosely matches the target text [19]. ASR self-verification has also\n184\tbeen used to filter stochastic failures in neural-codec TTS, including\n185\tsilence, early termination, repetition, and hallucinated content [20].\n186\tThese approaches demonstrate that useful variation among sampled\n187\tcandidates can be exploited at inference time.\n188\tVoDER-Cal follows this multi-candidate selection paradigm but\n189\toptimizes a different aspect of controllability. It first retains candidates that express the requested target response while preserving\n190\tcontent and speaker identity, and then favors candidates with smaller\n191\tmovement in non-target acoustic and prosodic measurements. Thus,\n192\trather than selecting candidates only for content reliability, VoDERCal uses inference-time variation to improve attribute preservation in\n193\tnatural-language voice control.\n194\t3. PAIRED EVALUATION OF ATTRIBUTE-LEVEL\n195\tCONTROL\n196\t\n197\t2.2. Evaluating controllability\n198\tControllable TTS systems are commonly evaluated using prompt or\n199\tstyle preference, naturalness, intelligibility, speaker similarity, and\n200\tselected acoustic correlates. Recent benchmarks more directly assess\n201\twhether generated speech follows complex natural-language instructions. InstructTTSEval organizes such instructions into a systematic\n202\t\n203\t3.1. Systems and generation matrix\n204\tWe evaluate CosyVoice3, VoxCPM2, and Fish-Speech-S2, three independently developed reference-conditioned speech-generation systems. Each system is tested using the same factorial matrix of six\n205\treference speakers, ten English texts, three random seeds, and eleven\n206\t\n207\t\fconditions: one neutral baseline, six voice descriptors, and four controls. This yields 1,980 outputs per system and 5,940 outputs overall.\n208\tThe primary cross-model audit and candidate-selection analysis\n209\tfocus on deep, bright, and rough, for which the evaluation protocol\n210\tdefines directly comparable signal-level target proxies. The remaining descriptors—warm, young, and professional—and the identitypreservation control are retained as supplementary audit conditions\n211\tand are not part of the primary claims.\n212\tEach descriptor-conditioned output is paired with a neutral baseline generated by the same system using the same reference speaker,\n213\ttext, and nominal random seed. This design allows descriptor-induced\n214\tchanges to be measured relative to a closely matched baseline. We\n215\tretain the native control interface of each system; in particular, FishSpeech-S2 uses its inline voice-tag syntax rather than a free-form\n216\tinstruction channel. Model identifiers and control interfaces are provided in the supplementary material.\n217\t3.2. Paired feature analysis\n218\tLet y0 denote a neutral baseline output and yd the paired output\n219\tconditioned on descriptor d. For acoustic or prosodic feature zj , the\n220\tpaired change is\n221\t∆zj = zj (yd ) − zj (y0 ).\n222\t(1)\n223\tWe measure pitch, timing, energy, spectral color, and signal\n224\tirregularity using F0, speaking rate, duration, RMS energy, spectral\n225\tcentroid, 85% spectral roll-off, spectral flatness, and zero-crossing\n226\trate. F0 changes are expressed in semitones. These are standard\n227\tsignal-level measurements rather than outputs of a learned attribute\n228\tevaluator [21, 22].\n229\tFor each descriptor, we define a signal-level target set and its\n230\texpected direction:\n231\tdeep : F0 ↓, rolloff ↓,\n232\tbright : centroid ↑, rolloff ↑,\n233\trough : flatness ↑, ZCR ↑ .\n234\t\n235\t(2)\n236\t(3)\n237\t(4)\n238\t\n239\tFeatures outside the corresponding target set are treated as offtarget measurements. These sets define the scope of the signal-level\n240\taudit rather than exhaustive perceptual models of the descriptors.\n241\tSpectral flatness and zero-crossing rate are limited proxies for roughness.\n242\tTo compare movements across features, we normalize absolute\n243\tchanges using fixed, physically interpretable scales shared across all\n244\tsystems. Because several measurements are correlated, counts of\n245\tshifted features describe the breadth of the measured response, not\n246\tthe number of independent perceptual attributes. We therefore report\n247\tboth individual movements and grouped summaries.\n248\t3.3. Validity checks and statistical inference\n249\tWe assess content preservation using normalized ASR transcripts\n250\tand speaker preservation using cosine similarity between generatedspeech and reference-speech ECAPA-TDNN embeddings; x-vector\n251\tsimilarity provides an additional cross-check [23, 24, 25]. Binary\n252\tcontent and speaker thresholds are determined on the calibration split\n253\tand then applied unchanged to the evaluation set. These measures\n254\tserve as validity checks for excluding cases dominated by content\n255\tcorruption or speaker replacement.\n256\tStatistical inference is performed at the request level, where a\n257\trequest is defined by a system, descriptor, reference speaker, and\n258\ttext. For mean feature effects, the three seed-level deltas are first\n259\taveraged within each request, and request blocks are then resampled\n260\t\n261\twith replacement. This avoids treating repeated generations of the\n262\tsame request as independent observations. Confidence intervals use\n263\t10,000 bootstrap replicates.\n264\tConditional-response proportions are computed at the output\n265\tlevel, but uncertainty is estimated by resampling request clusters.\n266\tOutputs sharing the same system, descriptor, speaker, and text are\n267\ttherefore kept together during resampling.\n268\t3.4. Conditional analysis and controls\n269\tTo distinguish collateral movement from failures to express the descriptor, we perform a second analysis conditioned on a clear target\n270\tresponse. Baseline variation across random seeds defines a one-sided\n271\ttarget-direction noise threshold for each descriptor. An output is\n272\tconsidered target-responsive when its target score exceeds this threshold. Among these outputs, we measure the fraction exhibiting at\n273\tleast one off-target change whose magnitude reaches half of its fixed\n274\tnormalization scale. Request-level bootstrap intervals are also used\n275\tto identify off-target features with systematic nonzero movement.\n276\tThe control set includes a neutral reading instruction, a nonsense descriptor, an identity-preservation instruction, and an explicit\n277\tlower-pitch instruction. The lower-pitch condition provides a positive\n278\tcontrol for the generation interface and F0 measurement. The neutral\n279\tand nonsense conditions quantify perturbations introduced by additional natural-language conditioning in the absence of a meaningful\n280\ttarget voice attribute.\n281\t4. VOICE DESCRIPTORS PRODUCE COUPLED CHANGES\n282\t4.1. Models respond, but not through isolated changes\n283\tFigure 2a shows that the three systems respond systematically to the\n284\ttested descriptors, but their responses frequently extend beyond the\n285\tcorresponding target features. For deep, outputs move in the expected\n286\ttarget direction in 84.4% of CosyVoice3 pairs, 77.2% of VoxCPM2\n287\tpairs, and 71.1% of Fish-Speech-S2 pairs. Responses to bright and\n288\trough vary more across systems and measured features.\n289\tTarget-aligned changes are rarely isolated. For example,\n290\tCosyVoice3 lowers F0 in response to deep, while also changing\n291\tspeaking rate, duration, RMS energy, spectral centroid, and roll-off.\n292\tIts bright response increases the target spectral measures but also\n293\tchanges speaking rate and energy. VoxCPM2 produces a strong\n294\tF0 reduction for deep together with lower RMS energy, whereas\n295\tits bright response is expressed more strongly through energy than\n296\tthrough the expected spectral targets. Overall, the same descriptor is\n297\trealized through different combinations of pitch, timing, energy, and\n298\tspectral change across systems.\n299\tTable 1 combines target responsiveness with content, speaker,\n300\tand off-target preservation measures. Content is preserved in at\n301\tleast 99.4% of outputs, and mean reference-speaker similarity ranges\n302\tfrom 0.821 to 0.902. The coupled acoustic changes therefore occur largely among outputs that retain both linguistic content and\n303\treference-speaker identity. Multiple off-target features show requestlevel confidence intervals excluding zero in most system–descriptor\n304\tsettings. These counts summarize the breadth of measured change.\n305\t4.2. Off-target changes remain after conditioning on response\n306\tConditioning on a clear target response does not remove off-target\n307\tchange. Across the eight system–descriptor settings with at least ten\n308\tabove-noise target-responsive outputs, 54.5%–95.8% of responsive\n309\toutputs exhibit at least one substantial off-target deviation (Fig. 2b).\n310\t\n311\t\f(a) Paired acoustic and prosodic shifts\n312\t-0.45*\n313\t\n314\t-0.20*\n315\t\n316\t-0.17*\n317\t\n318\t-0.15*\n319\t\n320\t-0.03\n321\t\n322\t-0.07*\n323\t\n324\t-0.47*\n325\t\n326\t+0.14*\n327\t\n328\tCosyVoice3 Bright\n329\t\n330\t+0.57*\n331\t\n332\t-0.54*\n333\t\n334\t+0.35*\n335\t\n336\t+0.16*\n337\t\n338\t+0.02\n339\t\n340\t+0.22*\n341\t\n342\t+0.55*\n343\t\n344\t+0.41*\n345\t\n346\tCosyVoice3 Rough\n347\t\n348\t-0.18*\n349\t\n350\t-0.29*\n351\t\n352\t-0.13*\n353\t\n354\t-0.13*\n355\t\n356\t-0.04\n357\